<Docs slug="adjust">
  <p>
    The panel on the right has three pages, picked from the icons on its
    edge &mdash; Adjustments, Crop &amp; Transform and Cleanup &mdash; and
    almost everything happens under <strong>Adjustments</strong>:
  </p>

  <ul class="lp-article-list">
    <li>
      <strong>White Balance</strong> &mdash; Temp and Tint, or click
      <strong>Pick Gray</strong> and then click a neutral-gray pixel in the
      photo to balance from it. Fastest way out of a yellow room.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Tone</strong> &mdash; Exposure, Contrast, Highlights, Shadows,
      Whites and Blacks. This is where most photos are won or lost.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Presence</strong> &mdash; Vibrance and Saturation. Vibrance
      leaves skin alone where it can, so try it before Saturation.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Detail</strong> &mdash; Denoise.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    <strong>Crop &amp; Transform</strong> straightens, crops and turns the
    photo in 90&deg; steps; the crop is kept when you move to another page.
  </p>

  <h2>Three habits that make it faster</h2>
  <ul class="lp-article-list">
    <li>
      <strong>Let the histogram start.</strong> <strong>Auto Adjust</strong>
      sets the tone sliders from the photo's own histogram, so you begin
      close and fine-tune from there. Run it on one photo from the Tone
      section, or on a whole selection from the bar under the grid &mdash;
      each photo is read from its own histogram. <strong>Reset</strong> puts
      the sliders back to zero.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Do one, then copy it.</strong> Photos from one shoot share the
      same light, so one good edit is usually most of the way for the rest.
      <strong>Copy Adjustment</strong> from a finished photo, select the rest
      of the shoot and <strong>Apply Adjustment</strong>. One good frame
      becomes the look for the shoot.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Heal the small stuff.</strong> Turn on <strong>Touch
      Up</strong> on the Cleanup page and click a blemish to remove it, up to
      64 spots per photo. It works from the full-resolution image, so give it
      a moment if you have just arrived from the grid.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    None of this touches the original file. Every slider is an instruction
    stored in the <code>.lightphotos</code> folder beside your photo, so you
    can close the app mid-edit, come back next week, or reset the whole photo
    without any harm done. When it looks right, the last step is
    <a href="/docs/export.html">export</a>.
  </p>
</Docs>
